meta {
  name: 10. Add UPI for Driver A (corrected endpoint)
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/vpas
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "vpa": "{{driverVpa}}",
    "driverId": "{{driverId}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("driverAVpaId", res.body.data.id);
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.success: eq true
  res.body.data.driverId: eq {{driverId}}
}

docs {
  Ensures driver A actually holds {{driverVpa}} before steps 11–14 test what happens when a
  second driver tries to claim it — see `folder.bru` for why this exists instead of relying on
  step 7. Calls `VPAResource.createVPA` (`onboarding/controller/VPAResource.java:79`), which is
  the endpoint the DRIVER role itself would use for this — here it's called with the admin token
  from step 9, which `VPAResource` also allows (`RolesAllowed({VAHN_ADMIN, TORG_ADMIN,
  FUND_MANAGER, TRUCK_MANAGER, DRIVER})`), and which doesn't check that the caller *is* driver A.

  `res.status` is 200, not 201, even though the resource returns `Response.status(CREATED)` —
  `ApiResponseFilter` rewrites every `api/*` 2xx response to a wire-level 200 with the real
  outcome in the body (`success`, `statusCode`). See `folder.bru`.

  If {{driverVpa}} already belongs to another driver from a previous partial run of this
  section, this comes back `success: false` — deactivate or reassign that row first (or change
  `driverVpa` in the environment) before re-running.
}
